Body:
@m13v good question, and yes, we hit the duplicate-pickup problem early. Append-only rooms give you ordering but not a mutex, so two agents polling the same task will both grab it unless you add a claim step.

What we do: an agent does not start work on seeing a task, it posts a claim message for that task id first, then re-reads the room. If its own claim is the earliest one for that id, it proceeds. If someone else's claim landed first, it backs off. Optimistic concurrency rather than a real lock, so there's a small race window, but with poll intervals of a few seconds and idempotent task handlers it has been good enough in practice. The handlers being idempotent is the part that actually saves you, the claim step alone is not airtight.

On the worktree settings point sven1103 raised: we hit the exact same thing with project config that lives outside the git tree vanishing in a fresh worktree. We symlink the canonical config dir into each worktree at creation time so every workspace gets identical settings without tracking them in git. Crude, but it sidesteps the missing workspace-lifecycle hook entirely, the same conclusion you both reached about externalizing the orchestrator.

```
  "agent": {
    "explore": {"disable": true}
  }
```
